3-The user's “one iteration” means one request to Codex should trigger a complete internal development cycle. It does **not** mean one code edit or one screenshot. Codex owns the repeated diagnosis and repair until all applicable gates pass. Do not transfer routine QA back to the user. A pass is evidence-based: there may be unknown bugs, but no known material mismatch, broken interaction, failing check, or unexamined requirement can be called done.
4-
5:Work on a visual slice small enough to compare with the reference, then integrate **every slice in the requested scope** into the full scroll page. A passed slice is an internal milestone, not a handoff. At the start of each slice, make a compact coverage record from the render: content/art, controls and destinations, viewport/locale states, and relevant speed risks. Give each applicable check a result of pass, fail, or blocked with evidence; do not equate “not yet tested” with pass. For every stage, keep the target, observation, defect, fix, and recheck clear. A failed gate routes work back to the responsible stage and reopens downstream checks affected by the change.
6-
7-Decide routine implementation details from the render, assets, code, and product brief without pausing for intermediate user approval. Reproduce visible reference copy in the local build for fidelity, with unverified factual claims tracked for publication. When a required media file, destination, credential, confirmed price/claim, or commerce behavior is genuinely absent, finish independent sections and checks, isolate the missing dependency, and ask only for the external input needed to finish that behavior or publish it. Do not invent it, silently omit a visible control, or mark that control complete. Keep a blocked gate distinct from a failed implementation that Codex can fix itself.
